                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "0ff8d6ad-85f7-45e6-a723-b89a4ebc4b42",
                        "source_entry_id": "a95d9ab7-c99c-3233-a77a-424fd0bd0131",
                        "source_sense_id": "414c810a-f1f6-38b8-953f-42881d009190",
                        "target_lang_word": "criteriologic",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "criteriologic",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "658d5dd3-1569-3666-9bbe-0a5394e28813",
                                        "entry_id": "30b13797-06e0-3333-8ee9-3e6ac196b5b1",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Pertaining to criteriology."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "42d249fd-ab50-4e65-aa66-3bf7a098309e",
                                                "sense_id": "658d5dd3-1569-3666-9bbe-0a5394e28813",
                                                "text": "In the course of these researches, he has clearly seen that psychoanalytic views have equally a religious and a criteriologic signification."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "96cf75f6-a784-488e-baf6-4e2b5126f9f6",
                                                "sense_id": "658d5dd3-1569-3666-9bbe-0a5394e28813",
                                                "text": "Thus we might ask Wittgenstein and Phillips if the structure of family resemblance does not dissolve the dichotomy of internal/external to which their semantic and criteriologic pragmatic contextualism is connected."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "e9ab9e4c-6a3e-4ddf-ba18-4fb3110d0284",
                                                "sense_id": "658d5dd3-1569-3666-9bbe-0a5394e28813",
                                                "text": "Frequently the writers forget explicitly the gnoseological, criteriologic, epistemological problem or the question of knowledge whatever its denomination is to explain how to reach knowledge in general and the juridical specifically."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "468866cc-f7e9-4a7e-bb63-d3a16782e90a",
                                                "sense_id": "658d5dd3-1569-3666-9bbe-0a5394e28813",
                                                "text": "The American Philosophical Association (APA) defined critical thinking as purposeful, self-regulatory judgement that uses cognitive tools such as interpretation, analysis, evaluation, inference, and explanation of the evidential, conceptual, methodologic, criteriologic, or contextual considerations on which judgment is based."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "64035a05-d21a-4dcc-8c27-4edb908ed91b",
                        "source_entry_id": "a95d9ab7-c99c-3233-a77a-424fd0bd0131",
                        "source_sense_id": "414c810a-f1f6-38b8-953f-42881d009190",
                        "target_lang_word": "criteriological",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "criteriological",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "629797d8-1081-30e2-9ee6-56f598452412",
                                        "entry_id": "2aa16926-5330-31ab-9681-d604093576cb",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Synonym of criteriologic."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "not-comparable"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3d1a8053-96fc-4baa-babf-d4b451d96886",
                                                "sense_id": "629797d8-1081-30e2-9ee6-56f598452412",
                                                "text": "This criteriological or normative aspect is what distinguishes the sciences of mind from the sciences of nature, which are said to be descriptive rather than normative."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ed46bb2a-0021-4516-8673-05bebe15f7df",
                                                "sense_id": "629797d8-1081-30e2-9ee6-56f598452412",
                                                "text": "Eidetic reduction, the more subjective (creative and risk-oriented) stage of eidetic analysis, operates without undisciplined subjectivism only through its criteriological principle."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "00330fea-89dc-464f-bfb8-d7fc82d8bbc2",
                                                "sense_id": "629797d8-1081-30e2-9ee6-56f598452412",
                                                "text": "This is the criteriological view of the development of an understanding of other minds. The criteriological view is summarized by Montgomery as follows: Wittgenstein's argument [is] that the presence of criteria is necessary for teaching the meanings of various mental state terms to children and, also, for then gauging children's correct use of these terms. Children use the criteria to conceptualize the mental state and to infer its presence or absence."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
